## What the licence does and does not cover

This licence covers **our** contribution: the structured records, the
classification, the normalisation, and the talent read-throughs.

It does **not** cover the underlying source material. Every record links to a
primary source (a press release, a regulatory filing, a news article) that
remains the property of its publisher under its own terms. We store a link, a
short factual summary, and our own analysis — never a reproduction of the
source. If you redistribute this data, you are responsible for how you use the
linked sources. Nor does it cover the upstream indexes that led us to those
sources, which are named below.

## Upstream sources

We do not redistribute anyone else's dataset. We redistribute our own
structured records and a link. But many of those records were *found* through
an upstream index rather than by reading a publisher directly, and those
indexes ask to be cited when work built on them is passed on:

- **GDELT** — https://www.gdeltproject.org/ — a global news index, and the only
  route we have to news older than a feed window. It gives us an article URL;
  we read the article and store the publisher.
- **SEC EDGAR** — https://www.sec.gov/edgar — US regulatory filings (8-K Item
  5.02 and Form D). The filings are public domain; the credit is a courtesy and
  a pointer to the authoritative copy.
- **Google News** — an aggregator used only for discovery. It is never stored
  as a source; the publisher it points to is.

The credit travels in the file, not only on our sources page: each exported
record names the index it was discovered through, so a downloaded CSV or JSON
carries everything needed to attribute it.

## Corrections

Records are event-sourced and never silently overwritten. If a record is wrong,
report it and the correction is published with its date, alongside the original.
